/*  abandonT -- A REFUSAL'S SCOPE IS THE STATEMENT. Tony, ruled 2026-09-17.

    ⚠ RETIRED-AND-REBUILT IN PLACE, AND THE OLD SUBJECT IS NAMED SO THE TRAIL SURVIVES.
    Until the statement boundary landed, a refusal was cleared only by aCTionDefinE's
    boundary or by runAction -- so one raised at statement level propagated, broke every
    enclosing block, and ABANDONED THE FILE at exit 0. This fixture pinned that. The
    boundary retired the behaviour, so the file pins the replacement with the same driver.

    ⚠ AB-1's COUNT IS THE WHOLE ASSERTION AND IT FLIPPED FROM 1 TO 2. One meant the file
    was abandoned at the refusal. Two means execution carried straight past it, which is
    what "scoped to the statement" MEANS. A 1 here is the old behaviour returning.

    ⚠ ABANDONED IS PINNED AT ZERO AND NEVER ALONE -- a zero is exactly what a REMOVED
    reporter also produces. AB-4 (`stop: end parsing`) is its non-zero sibling: it says
    the run reached its end properly rather than dying quietly.
    ⚠ AND THE HONEST STATE OF THAT REPORTER IS RECORDED RATHER THAN ASSERTED:
    reportRunAbandoned has NO REACHABLE CASE LEFT. Five shapes were tried on 2026-09-17 --
    refusal mid-file, as the final statement, inside a define as the final statement, in
    the last field of the last define, and this file's original shape -- and every one is
    cleared at a statement boundary, reading refused=0 at main's tail. It stands as a NET.

    ⚠ WHAT THIS FIXTURE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT USE IS A DEFINE. A refusal inside a define
    block still truncates the file, and MEASUREMENT SHOWS THAT IS NOT THE FLAG: with the
    boundary in place the run reaches main reading refused=0 and truncates anyway. It is
    the failed MATCH abandoning the parse -- ordinary parse-failure truncation, which is
    its own documented behaviour and its own row. Using a define here would make this
    fixture measure that instead of what it claims to measure.
    docs/refusalScope.md carries the argument.

    THE ROWS
      AB-1  the marker runs TWICE -- once before the refusal, once after
      AB-2  a plain statement after the refusal runs
      AB-3  the refusal itself, by message -- the anti-vacuity row for everything above,
            because a run with no refusal passes AB-1 and AB-2 trivially
      AB-4  `stop: end parsing` -- the run ended properly
      AB-5  ABANDONED count 0, read only with AB-4  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(AbandonT);
define
    abRoot;
    abMark code={ cerr "AB-1 marker ran":; };
    ;
abMark(abRoot);
abBad := abRoot.*abNope;
abMark(abRoot);
cerr "AB-2 a plain statement after the refusal RAN":;
stop();
